course: dictionary
lesson: 10203
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. Look at the words around it first. «The tip of the pencil snapped.» Here tip is the pointed end, because the sentence is about a pencil breaking.
explain: «Top tips for revision» uses tip in a different way. A tip is a small piece of useful advice, so the sentence means helpful ideas for studying. Ask yourself: does this meaning fit the sentence? If it does not, try another meaning.
explain: Tip can also be a doing word. «Tipping the head back is wrong» means tilting the head so it leans back. When tip is a doing word, look for an action in the sentence, and check the ending: tip, tips, tipped and tipping are all forms of the same word.
explain: Remember the UK meaning too. If someone says «The town tip was full of old fridges», a tip is a place where rubbish is taken. The spelling stays the same, so only the context tells you which meaning is right. When the clues are unclear, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Tip is a noun (a thing) and a verb (a doing word).
fact: «Tip» can mean the pointed or thin end of something, like a finger or a pencil.
fact: «Tip» can mean a small piece of useful advice, like «top tips for revision».
fact: «Tip» can mean extra money given to thank someone, like a waiter, and it can also mean to tilt something so it leans or falls over.
fact: In the UK, a tip is a place where rubbish is taken, or a very messy place.
exercise: «The tip of the pencil snapped in the middle of my sum.» What does tip mean here? | *The pointed end | A piece of advice | Extra money | A rubbish dump
exercise: «Top tips for revision will help you learn.» What does tip mean here? | To tilt something over | The thin end of a pen | *A small piece of useful advice | Money given to a waiter
exercise: «Tipping the head back is wrong, because blood runs down the throat.» What does tipping mean here? | Pouring out rubbish | Pointing at something | Giving extra money | *Tilting so the head leans back
test: «Please do not tip your chair back, it might fall over.» What does tip mean here? | The pointed end of the chair | A small piece of advice | *To tilt so it leans or falls | Money put in a tin
test: «The town tip was full of old fridges and broken chairs.» What does tip mean here? | A pointed end | Extra money for a waiter | Useful advice | *A place where rubbish is taken
exam: «Only the tip of the iceberg shows above the water.» What does tip mean here? | Helpful advice | *The top, pointed or thin part | To tilt over | Extra money to thank someone
